SYSTEM OVERVIEW
===============
The system is a Role-based Access Control (RBAC) environment supporting multiple users, role hierarchies, temporary role delegation, revocation, and concurrent request processing. The system enforces authorization correctness, maintains delegation and permission consistency, and ensures safety and liveness under concurrency. Users have clearance levels (High, Medium, Low), and objects have classification levels (High, Medium, Low). Requests are classified by the highest classification level of affected objects and must comply with user clearance. Higher-clearance users can approve or reject lower-clearance user requests. Delegation and revocation of roles are governed by clearance and administrative rules. The system includes an Emergency Module to manage exceptional scenarios, which alters standard behaviors under specific triggers and constraints. Emergency mode is abstracted as a fixed duration but may terminate early if both administrators who activated it jointly deactivate it. The system must eventually return to Normal mode after Emergency mode.

EXISTING-SYSTEM ASSUMPTIONS AND CONSTRAINTS
============================================
- The set of administrators can range from 0 to 4, depending on scenario. Administrator uniqueness is based on set semantics; duplicate entries are not possible.
- Emergency mode can only be triggered from Normal mode and requires exactly two distinct administrators, acting jointly (no more, no fewer).
- Emergency mode is time-bounded, abstracted as a fixed duration; it may terminate early if both participating administrators jointly deactivate Emergency. The system must eventually return to Normal mode after Emergency mode.
- FIFO ordering for request processing can be bypassed only during Emergency mode, and only for high-classification requests.
- Mutual exclusivity of roles is suspended for all users only during Emergency mode. During Emergency mode, any user may simultaneously hold any combination of roles, including roles that are mutually exclusive in Normal mode.
- Emergency privilege is granted only to the two administrators who triggered Emergency mode (emAdmins), only during Emergency mode, and is revoked immediately and always at the conclusion of Emergency mode. EmergencyPrivilege must never be granted outside Emergency mode.
- Roles delegated during Emergency mode must be revoked when Emergency mode ends; specifically, any delegation assigned during Emergency mode must end at the first Normal state after Emergency ends. Roles delegated prior to Emergency mode are not automatically revoked, but may still expire or be revoked by an administrator during Emergency mode.
- Only the administrators who participated in the Emergency trigger (the two emAdmins) are required to update their credentials after Emergency mode ends.
- No role is mutually exclusive with itself, and mutual exclusivity is symmetric.
- For all business scenarios, the set of administrators is bounded as above, but the system may contain a larger number of users for coverage and verification purposes.

PROSPECTIVE FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS
====================================
R1: Emergency Trigger  
Exactly two distinct administrators, while the system is in Normal mode, are required to jointly trigger entry into Emergency mode. No more and no fewer than two administrators may jointly trigger Emergency mode. Only these two participating administrators (emAdmins) are considered for all subsequent emergency-related requirements.

R2: Emergency Mode Duration  
Emergency mode is active only for a limited (bounded) time, abstracted as a fixed duration; however, it may terminate early if both participating administrators (emAdmins) jointly deactivate Emergency. The system must eventually return to Normal mode after Emergency mode.

R3: Emergency Mode Behavior  
R3.1. During Emergency mode, request processing must occur by classification: all high-classification requests are processed first (possibly concurrently), then all medium-classification requests are processed concurrently, and finally all low-classification requests are processed.  
R3.2. During Emergency mode, mutual exclusivity of roles is suspended for all users. It must be possible and observable for any user to simultaneously hold any combination of roles, including at least one pair of roles that are mutually exclusive in Normal mode.  
R3.3. During Emergency mode, only the two administrators who participated in the Emergency trigger (emAdmins) temporarily hold emergency privileges, and EmergencyPrivilege is granted only to them for the duration of Emergency mode. EmergencyPrivilege must never be granted outside Emergency mode and must always be revoked immediately after Emergency mode ends. The revocation time for each EmergencyPrivilege must be explicitly tracked.

R4: Post-Emergency Restoration  
R4.1. After Emergency mode ends, emergency privileges must be revoked from the two administrators (emAdmins) who participated in the Emergency trigger. The revocation time for each privilege must be explicitly recorded; emergency privilege must not persist into Normal mode, including the first step after Emergency mode ends.  
R4.2. After Emergency mode ends, any roles delegated during Emergency mode must be revoked; specifically:  
  - R4.2a. Any delegation assigned during Emergency mode must end at the first Normal state after Emergency ends.  
  - R4.2b. Delegated roles from before Emergency mode are not automatically revoked solely due to the transition out of Emergency mode; they must remain active until their explicit expiry or explicit revocation, including through and after Emergency mode, unless explicitly revoked.  
R4.3. After Emergency mode ends, only the two administrators who participated in the Emergency trigger are required to update their credentials.

R5: Delegation Clearance and Tracking  
R5.1. Users with higher clearance may temporarily delegate one or more of their assigned roles to a lower-clearance user, provided that the delegate has clearance greater than or equal to the role’s minimum clearance requirement.  
R5.2. Delegation can only occur if the delegator holds the role at the time of delegation, and the delegatee meets the clearance requirement at the time of delegation.  
R5.3. Self-delegation is prohibited; a user cannot delegate a role to itself.  
R5.4. Every delegation must include an explicit expiry time, and remains active until its expiry time unless revoked earlier. No delegation expires before it is granted.  
R5.5. All delegations and privilege requests must be tracked with the identity of the approver or denier.  
R5.6. There must be a record of assigned roles for each user so that delegation can be verified against assigned roles.

R6: Mutual Exclusivity Enforcement  
R6.1. In Normal mode, mutually exclusive roles must never be simultaneously assigned to the same user.  
R6.2. In Emergency mode, mutual exclusivity of roles is suspended for all users, and any user may simultaneously hold any combination of roles, including mutually exclusive pairs.

R7: Request/Delegation Approval Tracking  
R7.1. All privilege and delegation requests must be tracked with the identity of the user who approved or denied the request, and the decision made.

NEWLY DISCOVERED REQUIREMENT GAPS
==================================
- The following coverage conditions must be enforced globally during model checking at all times: (a) at least one Emergency state exists, (b) at least one pair of mutually exclusive roles exists, (c) at least one user simultaneously holds both roles of a mutually exclusive pair during Emergency mode, and (d) at least one delegation is granted before Emergency mode with expiry after Emergency mode ends.
- For verification, the system must always include users at all clearance levels (High, Medium, Low) in every scenario.
- For verification, the User scope must be set large enough to cover the maximum number of administrators (up to 4) and distinct users with different clearance levels, as required for coverage.
- For verification, mutually exclusive role pairs must exist at all times (except in non-Emergency verification scenarios, where their absence is permitted only if Emergency mode is not modeled).
- For verification, ensure inclusion of delegations granted before Emergency mode, with expiry after Emergency mode, to test persistence of such delegations through mode transitions.
- For each positive test scenario, scenario preconditions (such as Emergency present, required delegations present, role exclusivity present) must always be satisfied in verification runs.
- For verification, negative scenarios (such as self-delegation or delegator not assigned the role) are enforced via global facts; explicit negative scenario construction is not required if facts are sufficient.

PROVISIONAL ASSUMPTIONS FOR VERIFICATION
========================================
- For Alloy model checking, all coverage facts are enforced globally, not scenario-locally: (a) at least one Emergency state exists, (b) at least one pair of mutually exclusive roles exists, (c) at least one user is assigned two mutually exclusive roles during Emergency mode, and (d) at least one delegation spans Emergency mode (granted before, expires after).
- For verification, always include at least one user per clearance level (High, Medium, Low) in every run.
- The User scope should be increased as needed for verification to cover all coverage conditions, regardless of the fixed administrator count.
- For verification scenario naming, include `positive` or `negative` to clarify expected outcome and scenario coverage.
- No particular constraints are required on which roles are mutually exclusive for verification; mutual exclusion is abstract and not role-specific.
- Clearance level comparisons in Alloy must use appropriate techniques due to non-integer ordering; delegation clearance checks must use "greater than or equal to".
- Do not create redundant assertions that simply restate enforced facts; avoid vacuous assertions.
- For delegation expiry, model delegations so that each instance has an explicit expiry time, and persists until expiry unless revoked.
- Include edge-case scenarios, such as delegations with expiry after Emergency mode, and verify mutual exclusivity is only suspended in Emergency mode.
- Alloy syntax must not use a standalone `no`; always use `no` followed by an expression (e.g., `no x` or `no someRelation`). Negate conjunctions directly as needed.
